 ## Overview
-Spatially Constrained Optimization is a post-processing constraint workflow that applies an opacity loss to ensure full coverage of target objects, followed by the re-initialization of boundary primitives to clean up rendering artifacts [S1, unofficial]. It differs from applying opacity loss alone by specifically addressing the issue where primitives on object boundaries retain low opacity, which degrades qualitative results; adding re-initialization produces the best visual fidelity [S1, unofficial].
+Spatially Constrained Optimization is a post-processing constraint workflow that applies opacity loss to ensure full coverage of a target object, followed by the re-initialization of boundary primitives to clean up artifacts [S1, unofficial]. It solves the problem of incomplete object rendering and boundary artifacts that arise when optimizing for foreground-only appearance, distinguishing itself from standard pruning methods by retaining and reusing transparent primitives rather than removing them [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## How it works
